  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nA few showers occurred overnight and early this morning mostly over \nSan Diego and Orange County. North Island and Point Loma saw the \nhighest rainfall totals at 0.14\", further north and inland totals \nwere 0.10\" or less. Dry conditions are expected for the rest of the \nday through the rest of the week. Highs today will be a few degrees \nwarmer than Sunday, with an additional 3-5 degrees of warming into \nTuesday. \n\nThe transient ridge that is currently over the Western United States \nwill get pushed east Tuesday evening into Wednesday morning by an \nupper level low pressure system. This upper level low is not \nexpected to bring impactful weather to the area. Conditions will \nstay dry with just a minor cooling west of the mountains. \n\nFor Thursday, guidance has a ridge building back over California and \nthe aforementioned trough to our east. There still remains some \nuncertainty in the position of the upper level low, but guidance \ncontinues to indicate the pressure gradient will turn offshore. The \nprojected offshore surface gradient has trended slightly weaker \ncompared to yesterday, increasing the chances for mainly weak \nSanta Ana winds to develop. Guidance from the CW3E West-WRF has\ntrended slightly higher chances for weak Santa Ana winds and \nslightly lower chances for weak to moderate Santa Ana winds. Wind \ngusts look to peak in the mountains, foothills, and passes at 35 \nto 45 mph, with gusts to 45 mph confined to wind prone locations. \nThe pressure gradient is most offshore on Thursday, which is when \nthe strongest winds will be. In addition to winds, temperatures \nwill peak Thursday and Friday. High temperatures will be 5 to 15 \ndegrees above average, with the warmest conditions on Friday. West\nof the mountains, highs will be in the mid to upper 70s to \nmid-80s. \n\nBy Saturday, most ensemble solutions have a trough of low pressure \nstarting to push the ridge to our east. This will bring a few \ndegrees of cooling for Saturday compared to Friday. By Sunday, there \nremains a spread of solutions of where the low will be but 60% of \nensemble members continue to indicate the trough moving into \nNorthern California with the other 40% showing more zonal flow. The \ntroughing solutions are the ones most likely to bring us light \nprecipitation in Southern California. The current forecast follows \nthe NBM with chances of precipitation Sunday. A general troughing\npattern looks to prevail for much of next week, which has the \npotential to bring periods of additional precipitation, cooler \nconditions, and westerly winds for the first week of December.",
